{"id":"pay-jump-before-leaving","title":"Pay that jumped before an employee left the payroll","question":"Which City employees were paid far more in their last two years on the payroll than in their own earlier years, and what made up the rise?","finding":"Of 6,280 full-time employees who left the payroll between fiscal 2021 and 2025 and can be followed for the three years before their last two, 66 (1.1%) were paid at least 1.5 times their usual pay, and at least $40,000 more, in one of their last two years: it is uncommon. The rise was mostly overtime for 31, mostly regular salary for 22 and mostly other pay for 13. The pension fund’s plan summary and the payroll file both indicate that overtime does not count toward a San Francisco pension, so the overtime cases measure cash paid, not a larger pension; a rise in regular salary, as in the other 22 cases, is the kind that counts toward one.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2017 (the first year with names) to 2026. An employee “left” if named in a fiscal year and in no later one. Usual pay is the middle value of total pay in the three fiscal years before the last two; it must be at least $60,000, with full-time hours (a middle of 1,800 hours or more) in those three years. The peak is the higher-paid of the last two years. Listed: peak at least 1.5 times usual pay and at least $40,000 above it. The rise is split into regular salary, overtime and other pay, each against its own earlier middle value. The pension fund’s 2025 valuation says members pay contributions on “salary, excluding overtime and most premium pay”, and sets final pay as the highest average pay over one, two or three years depending on when the employee joined (for the oldest police and fire plans, the salary of the final rank held, or of the next lower rank if that rank was held for less than a year). The payroll file agrees on overtime. In fiscal 2025, in 16 of the 17 job classes large enough to compare, the City’s retirement payment was the same share of regular salary (within one percentage point) for full-time employees whose overtime was 30% or more of salary as for those with under 10%: 16.4% against 16.5% for the job “Transit Operator” and 16.2% against 16.4% for the job “Police Officer 3”. Had retirement been paid on overtime, the first share would be at least 30% larger than the second.","limits":"The payroll file does not record retirement: people who leave may have retired, resigned, died, moved to another employer or simply changed the spelling of their name. Employees are followed by name, so two people with one name can be merged (84 names with more hours than one person can work were dropped) and a name change breaks the trail. A rise in regular salary is usually a promotion or a move to a higher-paid post, which counts toward a pension under the fund’s rules. Overtime surges often reflect staff shortages, emergencies or a colleague’s absence. The file’s own notes describe “other pay” as “various irregular payments” including “premium pay, incentive pay, or other one-time payments”; it is not itemised, and a large one-time payment in a final year may be unused leave paid out on leaving. Nobody listed is accused of anything.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Employee","type":"person"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"job","t":"Job in peak year","type":"text"},{"k":"earlierJob","t":"Earlier job, if different","type":"text"},{"k":"lastYear","t":"Last fiscal year on payroll","type":"year"},{"k":"usualPay","t":"Usual pay in earlier years","type":"usd"},{"k":"peakPay","t":"Peak pay in last two years","type":"usd"},{"k":"times","t":"Times usual pay","type":"times"},{"k":"riseSalary","t":"Rise in regular salary","type":"usd"},{"k":"riseOvertime","t":"Rise in overtime","type":"usd"},{"k":"riseOther","t":"Rise in other pay","type":"usd"},{"k":"driver","t":"Mostly","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"person":"Anthony Montoya","department":"Police","job":"Sergeant 3","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2024,"usualPay":171271,"peakPay":383888,"times":2.24,"riseSalary":24322,"riseOvertime":168309,"riseOther":24185,"driver":"overtime"},{"person":"Ranon Ross","department":"District Attorney","job":"Manager V","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2025,"usualPay":118042,"peakPay":323530,"times":2.74,"riseSalary":54020,"riseOvertime":0,"riseOther":151467,"driver":"other pay"},{"person":"Michael Borovina-Jr","department":"Sheriff","job":"Senior Deputy Sheriff","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2025,"usualPay":364671,"peakPay":568084,"times":1.56,"riseSalary":17988,"riseOvertime":173544,"riseOther":26188,"driver":"overtime"},{"person":"Julie Mau","department":"Fire Department","job":"Assistant Deputy Chief 2","earlierJob":"Captain, Fire Suppression","lastYear":2025,"usualPay":259398,"peakPay":425894,"times":1.64,"riseSalary":23190,"riseOvertime":-33528,"riseOther":203844,"driver":"other pay"},{"person":"Xiaoqin Lu","department":"Public Health","job":"Registered Nurse","earlierJob":"Licensed Vocational Nurse","lastYear":2025,"usualPay":101382,"peakPay":265924,"times":2.62,"riseSalary":65993,"riseOvertime":85095,"riseOther":12395,"driver":"overtime"},{"person":"Jarrod Yee","department":"Police","job":"Sergeant 3","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2024,"usualPay":230279,"peakPay":391320,"times":1.7,"riseSalary":9499,"riseOvertime":134100,"riseOther":3314,"driver":"overtime"},{"person":"Thorin Shattuck","department":"Fire Department","job":"Captain, Fire Suppression","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2024,"usualPay":209085,"peakPay":367909,"times":1.76,"riseSalary":28386,"riseOvertime":15045,"riseOther":117963,"driver":"other pay"},{"person":"Kenneth Cofflin","department":"Fire Department","job":"Assistant Deputy Chief 2","earlierJob":null,"lastYear":2025,"usualPay":290710,"peakPay":443436,"times":1.53,"riseSalary":-75876,"riseOvertime":0,"riseOther":228603,"driver":"other pay"},{"person":"Wendi Boselli","department":"Child Support Services","job":"Attorney (Civil/Criminal)","earlierJ